1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,692
What may you keep about a card?

2
00:00:02,742 --> 00:00:08,076
When your bank calls you, they say: your card ending seven eight nine zero.

3
00:00:08,126 --> 00:00:11,923
They never ask for the three digits on the back.

4
00:00:11,973 --> 00:00:19,615
Now picture a support engineer who pastes a whole card number into a ticket, to check it with the bank.

5
00:00:19,665 --> 00:00:26,538
The number now lives in the ticket's backups, in its search index, and with everyone who reads tickets.

6
00:00:26,588 --> 00:00:29,615
One pasted number lives everywhere the ticket goes.

7
00:00:29,665 --> 00:00:33,461
Systems should talk about cards the way your bank does.

8
00:00:33,511 --> 00:00:40,384
The long number on the front of the card is the P A N, the card's own number.

9
00:00:40,434 --> 00:00:45,769
It lives in one place only, the card vault, and lesson four shows why.

10
00:00:45,819 --> 00:00:51,538
The code on the back is never kept by anyone once a payment is approved.

11
00:00:51,588 --> 00:00:54,615
And this system never even asks for it.

12
00:00:54,665 --> 00:01:01,923
Screens and logs may show the masked number: the first six digits and the last four, with nothing between.

13
00:01:01,973 --> 00:01:05,769
That is your bank's card ending seven eight nine zero.

14
00:01:05,819 --> 00:01:11,538
The first digits only tell which bank issued the card, and lesson ten reads them.

15
00:01:11,588 --> 00:01:13,846
Other systems keep a card token.

16
00:01:13,896 --> 00:01:18,846
A card token is a random name the card vault gives the card.

17
00:01:18,896 --> 00:01:25,384
Nothing in it comes from the number, so the token tells a thief nothing about the card.

18
00:01:25,434 --> 00:01:28,846
In lesson four, the token becomes a coat-check ticket.

19
00:01:28,896 --> 00:01:31,153
Now back to the support ticket.

20
00:01:31,203 --> 00:01:36,923
It should have held the masked number or the token, never the P A N.

21
00:01:36,973 --> 00:01:40,384
Now make both yourself, the way the code does.

22
00:01:40,434 --> 00:01:45,769
The card vault keeps two numbers: six digits shown first, and four shown last.

23
00:01:45,819 --> 00:01:50,769
A token is a short prefix plus sixteen random bytes, written in hex.

24
00:01:50,819 --> 00:01:55,769
Open J shell, which comes with Java, and take a made-up card number.

25
00:01:55,819 --> 00:02:00,769
Keep the first six and the last four, and star out the rest.

26
00:02:00,819 --> 00:02:05,000
Then draw sixteen random bytes, and write them after the prefix.

27
00:02:05,050 --> 00:02:11,153
Run it again, and you get a different token, with nothing from the number in either.

28
00:02:11,203 --> 00:02:21,538
Your bank never asks for the code on the back, and our systems never hold the P A N: they hold the masked number and the token.

29
00:02:21,588 --> 00:02:24,230
Next: where a card number may live.
